WEBVTT

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.150
You want a league table for eight schools.

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:07.330
The results are spread across eight websites, on pages made for people,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.750
not programs.

00:00:10.294 --> 00:00:12.534
To collect them, you write a scraper:

00:00:12.654 --> 00:00:17.094
a program that downloads those pages and pulls rows out of them.

00:00:17.614 --> 00:00:19.814
The simplest one is a single loop.

00:00:21.414 --> 00:00:25.714
For each site: download the page, find the results table, read each

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:28.094
row, and add it to the standings.

00:00:28.374 --> 00:00:30.334
On a good day, it works.

00:00:30.774 --> 00:00:34.254
Then, one at a time, five ordinary things go wrong.

00:00:36.294 --> 00:00:38.774
First: one site doesn't answer.

00:00:39.054 --> 00:00:43.114
The loop waits, gives up, and crashes, taking the finished sites

00:00:43.134 --> 00:00:43.894
down with it.

00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:48.282
So downloading becomes its own stage:

00:00:48.322 --> 00:00:52.622
scrape. It works down a list of addresses, checks whether each site

00:00:52.642 --> 00:00:55.162
answered, waits, and tries again.

00:00:55.602 --> 00:00:59.562
A silent site is now scrape's to deal with, instead of a crash.

00:01:00.242 --> 00:01:03.082
What it should do about one is a real decision.

00:01:03.202 --> 00:01:04.362
Hold on to that.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.410
Second: a month later, you find a bug.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.690
Your code had home and away scores backwards.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.470
You fix it and go back for the pages, but the sites have changed,

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.930
and some of last month's pages are gone.

00:01:20.578 --> 00:01:21.978
That brings in store.

00:01:22.338 --> 00:01:26.238
Save every page exactly as it arrived, before any of your code reads

00:01:26.258 --> 00:01:28.378
it. Now a bug is cheap.

00:01:28.658 --> 00:01:32.038
Fix the code and run it again on the saved pages, without asking

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:33.418
the web for anything.

00:01:36.334 --> 00:01:37.894
Third, and quieter:

00:01:38.174 --> 00:01:42.614
a site is redesigned, and a ticket table now sits above the results.

00:01:43.054 --> 00:01:47.034
The loop reads the first table it finds, the wrong one, and nothing

00:01:47.054 --> 00:01:48.054
complains.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.370
Reading the page becomes its own stage:

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.970
parse. It finds the schedule by its label, not its position, and

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.510
hands on rows in one fixed shape:

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.630
date, opponent, result.

00:02:05.562 --> 00:02:07.842
Fourth: put the rows together.

00:02:08.202 --> 00:02:13.842
A match between A and B is listed on A's page, and again on B's.

00:02:14.042 --> 00:02:17.922
Add up every row, and every match counts twice.

00:02:19.554 --> 00:02:22.714
That forces build, where rows become matches.

00:02:22.994 --> 00:02:26.334
It decides which copy of each match to keep, and only then works

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:27.434
out the standings.

00:02:27.794 --> 00:02:30.554
Which copy it keeps is another quiet choice.

00:02:30.754 --> 00:02:32.234
Hold on to that one, too.

00:02:34.056 --> 00:02:36.926
Fifth: the run ends and hands you a table.

00:02:37.686 --> 00:02:39.246
Did every site answer?

00:02:39.366 --> 00:02:40.846
How many rows came in?

00:02:41.206 --> 00:02:42.366
Were there errors?

00:02:42.886 --> 00:02:44.206
The table can't say.

00:02:45.822 --> 00:02:47.302
That is report's job.

00:02:47.662 --> 00:02:49.542
It writes down what the run did:

00:02:49.582 --> 00:02:53.942
the pages it fetched, the rows it found, and the errors it hit.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.850
Line the five stages up, and one loop has become a pipeline.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.130
Scrape hands pages to store.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:06.650
Store hands saved pages to parse.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:09.690
Parse hands rows to build.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.170
Build makes the table, and hands its counts to report.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:18.670
Each stage can be checked on its own, and run again without starting

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:19.290
over.

00:03:22.206 --> 00:03:23.926
Now read that report again.

00:03:24.446 --> 00:03:28.806
Eight pages fetched, one hundred and four rows, no errors.

00:03:29.166 --> 00:03:31.526
Every line is about what the run did.

00:03:31.886 --> 00:03:34.326
Not one says what the run should have found.

00:03:34.686 --> 00:03:37.686
That gap is what this module is about.

